        "avatarURL": "https://canny.io/images/52e5f1b9a956b64eade4fece763f090f.png",
        "created": "2018-01-04T21:20:46.279Z",
        "name": "Joshuarox100",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": "<redacted>"
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "5ed571497ee72a2434704a15",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2020-09-13T02:36:46.879Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "5f4ec7fc4b22df01530d2055",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5f3eb972eef9b549db40ba3a",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Ok, one final update on this topic.\nI have managed to both find the place this mistake is located at within the VRCSDK builder code and have successfully fixed the debug menu myself.\nMy theory about the use of Layer names rather than State Machine names was correct. As can be seen within [this image](https://imgur.com/2VDVdBj), they use the Layer names when generating hashes and ignore Sub-State Machines entirely. This section of code can be found within \"VRCSdkControlPanelAvatarBuilder.cs\" at line 343 within the VRCSDK.\nTo fix it, I created a separate static class named AV3Hasher and put in a recursive method for hashing an entire State Machine properly. I then simply replaced the code within the foreach loop in the SDK with a call to this function. You can see an image of the class' structure [here](https://imgur.com/j6VKl4G) and how the code in the VRCSDK was changed [here](https://imgur.com/mdImWX7).\nThe Debug menu shows all State names correctly upon uploading an Avatar that uses Sub-State Machines and differently named State Machines after doing this modification, signifying that this entire issue can be completely resolved with an update to the SDK.\nHowever, one thing I noticed when testing this fix is that any Layers that weren't customized (using defaults) will still be using incorrect hashes, meaning that the hashes for default layers are stored elsewhere and need to be updated as well.\nIf any VRChat developers are reading this and would like to incorporate this code into the SDK themselves, I've created a Unity Package  containing both the AV3Hasher class and the modified \"VRCSdkControlPanelAvatarBuilder.cs\" file that you can download [here](https://mega.nz/file/RQgH3LDL#Gmc0CPAZdWc0Sv5ZaARdM3hcCABzXG0oVPtmVoipELY).",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
